<body class="marketing-page">
	<SiteNav />

	<main>

	<section class="hero alt-hero">
		<div class="hero-badge">Alternatives · Self-Hosted Email</div>
		<h1>Google Workspace alternative.<br/><span>Own your email, don't rent it per seat.</span></h1>
		<p class="alt-lead">
			Vectis Mail is a self-hosted email server you run on your own VPS: full
			IMAP/SMTP mailboxes on your domain, a REST API, DKIM/SPF/DMARC by default,
			flat pricing that never scales with headcount. The honest caveat up front:
			Vectis Mail replaces the <strong>email</strong> half of Google Workspace —
			Gmail and your mail server — not the whole suite. There is no Docs, Sheets,
			Meet, or Drive here. If your team lives in those, keep them or pair Vectis
			Mail alongside. If you mostly want to own your email and stop paying per user
			forever, this is the switch.
		</p>
		<div class="alt-meta">
			<span>Last updated <time datetime={dateModified}>21 June 2026</time></span>
			<span>·</span>
			<img class="byline-avatar" src="/brand/imagery/ian-holt-avatar.webp" alt="" width="24" height="24" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><span>Written by <a href="/about/" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px">Ian Holt</a></span>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 2rem;">
		<h2>The short answer</h2>
		<p class="subtitle">When Vectis Mail wins, and the one thing it deliberately doesn't do.</p>
		<p>
			Google Workspace is a productivity <em>suite</em> — Gmail plus Docs, Sheets,
			Slides, Drive, Meet, and Calendar — billed per user per month. Vectis Mail is
			an email <em>server</em>, billed flat. They overlap on exactly one thing:
			hosting your company mailboxes. Where they overlap, Vectis Mail wins on price
			(flat, not per-seat), on data custody (your server, not Google's cloud), and on
			lock-in (plain IMAP/SMTP on your own domain, so leaving is trivial). Where they
			<em>don't</em> overlap — collaborative documents and video meetings — Vectis Mail
			has nothing, and that's by design. The right move for most teams that switch is
			to move mailboxes to Vectis Mail and either drop the suite or keep document and
			video tools running separately.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>At a glance</h2>
		<p class="subtitle">Side-by-side on the dimensions that actually decide the call. Current as of June 2026.</p>
		<div class="compare-table-wrap">
			<table class="compare-table">
				<thead>
					<tr><th></th><th>Vectis Mail</th><th>Google Workspace</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><th scope="row">What it is</th><td>Self-hosted <strong>email server</strong></td><td>Cloud productivity <strong>suite</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Pricing model</th><td><strong>Flat</strong> — $0 self-host / $39 per tenant/mo</td><td><strong>Per user / month</strong> (~$7–$22/user)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Scales with headcount?</th><td><strong>No</strong> — same price at 10 or 500 mailboxes</td><td>Yes — linear with every seat</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Where your mail lives</th><td><strong>Your server / your Postgres + IMAP store</strong></td><td>Google's cloud (US-primary)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Mail protocols</th><td><strong>Standard IMAP / POP3 / SMTP</strong></td><td>IMAP/SMTP supported; ecosystem-optimised for Gmail</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Lock-in</th><td><strong>None</strong> — plain IMAP on your domain</td><td>Suite + admin + identity ecosystem</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Email on your domain</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Docs / Sheets / Slides</th><td><strong>No</strong> (email only)</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Meet / video</th><td><strong>No</strong></td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Drive / Calendar</th><td>No (CalDAV on Phase-4 roadmap)</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">REST API + inbound webhooks</th><td><strong>Yes</strong> — domain-scoped keys, HMAC-signed</td><td>Gmail API exists; no flat-priced send API</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Deliverability ownership</th><td><strong>You</strong> — your IP, your reputation to warm</td><td>Google — pre-warmed, managed</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Spam filtering</th><td>Rspamd + optional ClamAV (you tune it)</td><td>Google's scale (very strong)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Ops burden</th><td>You run a VPS</td><td>Zero — fully managed</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">SSO</th><td>OIDC (Pro) · SAML 2.0 (Enterprise)</td><td>Built-in Google identity + SAML</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--sl-color-gray-3); margin-top: 0.5rem;">
			Google Workspace plan prices per <a href="https://workspace.google.com/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">workspace.google.com/pricing</a> (retrieved June 2026); annual-commit per-user/month. Vectis Mail Starter ($0) covers up to 3 domains and 25 mailboxes/domain; Pro is $39 USD/tenant/month plus a $5–$20/mo VPS.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>The verdict</h2>
		<div class="verdict-grid">
			<div class="verdict-card verdict-win">
				<h3>Choose Vectis Mail when…</h3>
				<ul>
					<li>Your Workspace bill grows every time you hire, and a flat price that ignores headcount would change the math.</li>
					<li>Data custody or jurisdiction matters — you want mail in your own Postgres and IMAP store under your chosen jurisdiction, not on Google's US-primary cloud under an attestation.</li>
					<li>You want zero lock-in: plain IMAP/SMTP on your own domain means leaving is a DNS change, not a migration project.</li>
					<li>You need a real transactional sending API + inbound webhooks alongside human mailboxes — one platform for both.</li>
					<li>You already use non-Google tools for documents and meetings (or don't need them), so the suite isn't load-bearing.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="verdict-card verdict-stay">
				<h3>Stay on Google Workspace when…</h3>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>Your team genuinely lives in the suite.</strong> Real-time Docs/Sheets co-editing and Google Meet are core to how you work. Vectis Mail is email only — it does not and will not replace those.</li>
					<li><strong>You have nobody to run a VPS.</strong> Self-hosting means you own deliverability, IP warmup, and uptime. If "never think about mail infrastructure" is worth the per-seat premium, Workspace earns it.</li>
					<li><strong>You're a non-technical org that wants one vendor for everything.</strong> One bill, one admin console, one support line across mail, docs, and video has real value.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="feature-card" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<h3>The honesty that builds trust</h3>
			<p style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;"><strong>"Doesn't Google read my email to sell ads or train AI?"</strong> For <em>paid</em> Workspace, Google's stated policy is that customer data is not used for advertising or to train its generative AI models, and Gemini interactions stay inside your Workspace tenant. That's the consumer-Gmail reputation, not the paid-business reality — so we won't pretend otherwise. The real argument for self-hosting isn't "Google snoops." It's <em>custody and jurisdiction</em>: do you want to hold the data yourself, or trust a policy you can't inspect?</p>
			<p><strong>"Will my mail still land if I host it myself?"</strong> Day one on a fresh VPS IP, you need 2–4 weeks of <a href="/deliverability/" style="color: var(--sl-color-accent);">IP warmup</a> before sending volume to Gmail/Outlook inboxes — Google hides this because its IPs are already warm. You don't take a day-one hit migrating: the dual-send playbook keeps Workspace delivering while your new IP warms. After warmup, a correctly configured self-hosted IP with DKIM/SPF/DMARC/MTA-STS is functionally on par for inbox placement.</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>How they differ</h2>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Own your email instead of renting it per seat</h3>
		<p>
			Google Workspace prices per user per month. As of early 2026, Business Starter
			is about $7/user/month on an annual plan ($8.40 billed monthly), Business
			Standard about $14/user/month ($16.80 monthly), and Business Plus about
			$22/user/month ($26.40 monthly). Google raised prices roughly 17–22% in 2025
			when it bundled Gemini AI into the paid plans. The bill is linear: every new
			hire is another line item, forever.
		</p>
		<p>
			Vectis Mail prices per tenant — your organisation — not per seat. Starter is
			free forever (up to 3 domains, 25 mailboxes per domain). Pro is $39 USD per
			tenant per month for unlimited domains and unlimited mailboxes, plus a
			$5–$20/month VPS to host it. The total is flat whether you run 10 mailboxes or
			500. One Pro subscription covers every Vectis Mail install your organisation
			operates.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">The TCO math at 10 and 50 users</h3>
		<div class="compare-table-wrap">
			<table class="compare-table">
				<thead>
					<tr><th>Mailboxes</th><th>Google Workspace (Business Standard, annual)</th><th>Vectis Mail (Pro + $20/mo VPS)</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><th scope="row">10 users</th><td>~$140/mo · ~$1,680/yr</td><td><strong>$59/mo flat · $708/yr</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">50 users</th><td>~$700/mo · ~$8,400/yr</td><td><strong>$59/mo flat · $708/yr</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">200 users</th><td>~$2,800/mo · ~$33,600/yr</td><td><strong>$59/mo flat · $708/yr</strong></td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--sl-color-gray-3); margin-top: 0.5rem;">
			Workspace figures use Business Standard at ~$14/user/month (annual commit), retrieved June 2026. The honest counter: that Workspace spend also buys Docs, Sheets, Meet, Drive, and zero operational burden. Vectis Mail is email only — so the fair comparison is "email versus email," with the suite priced separately if you still need it.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Email vs the whole suite — the caveat that matters most</h3>
		<p>
			This is the line we won't blur. Google Workspace is Gmail <em>plus</em> Docs,
			Sheets, Slides, Drive, Meet, and Calendar. Vectis Mail is the email server and
			nothing else. There is no document editor, no spreadsheet, no video meeting, no
			shared Drive. If your organisation's day runs on real-time collaborative
			documents or Google Meet, swapping in Vectis Mail does not replace that, and
			pretending it does would waste your time.
		</p>
		<p>
			So if you switch, plan for it honestly. Three realistic shapes:
			(1) <strong>email-only move</strong> — your team already uses other tools for
			docs/calls, so you simply move mailboxes to Vectis Mail and drop Workspace;
			(2) <strong>keep the suite, move the mail</strong> — you stay on a cheaper
			plan or another suite for docs/video but reclaim email custody and flat
			pricing; (3) <strong>pair with open tools</strong> — Vectis Mail for mail,
			plus something like Nextcloud/OnlyOffice for documents and Jitsi for video.
			Calendar/contacts (CalDAV/CardDAV) is on the Vectis Mail Phase-4 roadmap, not
			shipping today.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Data custody and jurisdiction</h3>
		<p>
			With Workspace, your mail lives on Google's infrastructure (US-primary) under
			Google's terms. Google's stated policy for paid Workspace is that it does not
			use your content for advertising or to train its generative AI — that's real,
			and we won't tell you otherwise. But it's still a policy you trust rather than a
			boundary you control.
		</p>
		<p>
			With Vectis Mail, the data is wherever you deploy the server. Your Postgres
			holds the metadata and audit trail; your filesystem holds the IMAP message
			store. GDPR, data-residency, and sovereignty conversations become "which region
			did you deploy in" instead of "do you accept the vendor's attestation." For
			regulated industries and EU public-sector buyers, that shift is frequently the
			whole reason to self-host.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">No lock-in: plain IMAP/SMTP on your domain</h3>
		<p>
			Workspace is sticky by design — the admin console, identity, Drive sharing, and
			the suite all reinforce staying. Vectis Mail is the opposite: mailboxes are
			vanilla Dovecot over standard IMAP/POP3, sending is standard SMTP, and the
			address is on <em>your</em> domain. Migrating in or out is an <code>imapsync</code>
			run and a DNS change, not a project. You are never holding the data hostage to
			keep a customer, and neither are we.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Admin, API, and full control</h3>
		<p>
			Workspace gives you a polished admin console for a managed service. Vectis Mail
			gives you the keys to the stack: a React admin UI, a 40+ endpoint REST API
			(sending, mailboxes, domains, webhooks, analytics) with domain-scoped keys, a
			single declarative <code>config.yaml</code> as the source of truth, and
			atomic updates with automatic rollback. You can tune Postfix, write custom
			Rspamd rules, and automate everything via the API or Terraform — control that a
			managed suite, by definition, doesn't hand you.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Deliverability — the real trade-off</h3>
		<p>
			This is where Google has the structural edge, and we'll say so plainly. Google
			operates warm IP pools and runs ISP relations at planetary scale, so Workspace
			mail "just lands." Self-hosting on a fresh VPS IP means your IP has no
			reputation on day one; Gmail, Outlook, and Yahoo will throttle or spam-fold most
			mail until you've established a pattern over 2–4 weeks. Vectis Mail ships DKIM,
			SPF, DMARC, and MTA-STS configured correctly by default, and the
			<a href="/deliverability/">dual-send migration playbook</a> means you keep
			Workspace delivering while your new IP warms — so there's no day-one gap, just
			an overlap window you control. After warmup, a correctly run self-hosted IP is
			functionally on par for inbox placement.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Migrating from Google Workspace to Vectis Mail</h2>
		<p>
			The mailbox move is mechanical; the constraint is the IP warmup calendar (and,
			if you use the suite, deciding what replaces Docs/Meet). The typical sequence:
		</p>
		<ol class="alt-steps">
			<li><strong>Decide the suite question first.</strong> Confirm what replaces Docs, Sheets, Meet, and Drive — keep them, switch to open tools, or accept you don't need them. Vectis Mail handles only email.</li>
			<li><strong>Stand up Vectis Mail</strong> on a fresh VPS via the <a href="/getting-started/installation/">installation guide</a>. Add your domain(s) and publish the DKIM/SPF/DMARC records Vectis Mail generates.</li>
			<li><strong>Sync mailbox data</strong> with <code>imapsync</code> from Gmail/Workspace IMAP into Vectis Mail's Dovecot — messages, folders, and flags transfer over standard IMAP.</li>
			<li><strong>Run in parallel during warmup.</strong> Keep Workspace receiving while your new IP warms; cut sending over gradually following the IP warmup schedule.</li>
			<li><strong>Cut MX over</strong> to the Vectis Mail server once warm. Mail starts flowing through Vectis Mail as DNS propagates.</li>
			<li><strong>Verify + decommission.</strong> Watch logs and deliverability for a week, then close down Workspace mail (and keep or cancel the suite per step 1).</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			Realistic timeline: a few hours of setup, then a 2–4 week warmup window that
			runs in the background while Workspace keeps serving. See the
			<a href="/guides/deliverability/">deliverability guide</a> for the calendar.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
		<div class="alt-faq">
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is there a self-hosted alternative to Google Workspace?</h3>
				<p>
					For the email half, yes — Vectis Mail. It's a self-hosted email server
					(Postfix, Dovecot, Rspamd) you run on your own VPS, with full IMAP/SMTP
					mailboxes on your domain, a REST API, inbound webhooks, and
					DKIM/SPF/DMARC configured by default. Pricing is flat: <a href="/pricing/">$0
					self-hosted Starter, or $39/month per tenant for Pro</a> — never per user,
					never per email. The honest caveat: it replaces Gmail and your mail server,
					not the whole suite. No Docs, Sheets, Slides, Drive, Meet, or Calendar. If
					your team lives in those, keep them or pair Vectis Mail alongside.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Can Vectis Mail replace Gmail?</h3>
				<p>
					Yes. Vectis Mail hosts full IMAP/POP3/SMTP mailboxes on your own domain with
					Roundcube webmail included, so users keep <code>name@yourdomain.com</code>
					and connect from Outlook, Apple Mail, Thunderbird, or any mobile client over
					standard protocols. It's the Gmail half of Workspace on your server. What you
					trade away is Google's spam-filtering scale and the deep Gmail
					search/label UX; what you gain is data custody, a flat price, and zero
					ecosystem lock-in.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does Vectis Mail replace Google Docs, Sheets, and Meet?</h3>
				<p>
					No — and this is the point. Vectis Mail is <strong>email only</strong>. No
					Docs, Sheets, Slides, Drive, Meet, or Calendar, and it doesn't try to be
					them. Google Workspace is a full productivity suite; Vectis Mail replaces the
					email server inside it. If you depend on real-time collaborative documents or
					Google Meet, either keep Workspace for everyone, keep the suite and move only
					mailboxes to Vectis Mail, or pair Vectis Mail with separate document/video
					tools (Nextcloud, OnlyOffice, Jitsi). Choose Vectis Mail when email
					sovereignty and flat pricing outweigh a single bundled suite.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>How much does Google Workspace cost versus self-hosting email?</h3>
				<p>
					Workspace bills per user per month. As of early 2026: Business Starter ~$7/user/mo
					annual ($8.40 monthly), Business Standard ~$14/user/mo ($16.80 monthly),
					Business Plus ~$22/user/mo ($26.40 monthly) — linear with headcount. Vectis
					Mail is flat: $0 Starter or $39/month per tenant for Pro, plus a $5–$20/month
					VPS. At 50 users, Business Standard is roughly $700/month ($8,400/year); the
					equivalent Vectis Mail Pro install is about $59/month all-in. The fair caveat:
					that Workspace price also buys Docs, Meet, Drive, and zero ops — a self-hosted
					email server doesn't.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does Google use my Workspace email to train its AI?</h3>
				<p>
					For paid Workspace, Google's stated policy is no — customer data isn't used to
					train its generative AI or for advertising, and Gemini interactions stay inside
					your tenant. That's the contractual reality, not the consumer-Gmail reputation,
					and we won't overstate it. The real self-hosting argument is custody and
					jurisdiction: with Workspace your mail lives on Google's US-primary
					infrastructure under a policy you trust; with Vectis Mail it sits in your own
					Postgres and IMAP store wherever you deploy, so the question becomes "where did
					you host it," not "do you trust the vendor's attestation."
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>When does Google Workspace make more sense than self-hosting?</h3>
				<p>
					Keep Workspace when your team genuinely lives in the suite (real-time
					Docs/Sheets and Meet are core), when you have nobody to run a VPS and
					"never think about mail infrastructure" is worth the per-seat premium, or
					when you're a non-technical org that wants one vendor for everything.
					Self-hosting means you own deliverability, IP warmup, and uptime. Vectis Mail
					wins on flat cost, data custody, and zero lock-in; Workspace wins on the
					bundled suite and zero operations. Be honest about which you actually need.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

